\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The inversion product of the free Wilson kernel}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{In \ref{fdip-01AP} take even periods, and use the [graded total inversion](fdip-019C) #{(U_-\psi)(x)=C\psi(-x)}. This map commutes with the Wilson kernel at trivial links by \ref{fdip-019F} and preserves #{C}, hence commutes with #{H=C(D_{W,a,U}-M\bbI)}. It commutes with the time map there since the coordinate reflections commute and the time map fixes #{C}. At a self-opposite momentum the site pullback preserves the character, so total inversion acts by #{C} on the values.}

\p{The 4 self-opposite labels have each component #{0} or #{\pi}. At one with #{j} components equal to #{\pi}, the centered term vanishes and #{H=(2j-aM)C/a}. Each of the two #{C}-eigenspaces is a spin line tensored with the two-dimensional fiber. If #{0<aM<2}, the negative spectral subspace at the origin has inversion sign #{+1}, and those at the other three labels have sign #{-1}. Each is exactly one antiunitary pair by \ref{fdip-01AO}. The [inversion product](fdip-01AV) is #{-1}. If #{aM<0}, all 4 signs are #{-1} and the product is #{+1}.}
